4. MERGE COMPOSITION — PASS (no third classifier; overlap counted once)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Read from source (handlers.go:725-728): the merged set is
    append(append([]models.Transaction{}, livingOutflows.Transactions...),
                                          healthcareOutflows.Transactions...)
where livingOutflows comes from `metrics.LivingOutflows(outflows,
planExclusions)` (attempt 3's single exclusion implementation) and
healthcareOutflows from the pre-existing
`outflows.FilterByCategory(metrics.HealthInsuranceCategory)`. Two
already-classified sets concatenated — no new predicate, no third
classifier, satisfying the split-classification rule structurally.

I did not leave that as a reading. My probe counts, per transaction Hash,
how many times each row lands in the merged bucket, on a fixture containing
all four row kinds including the HI+flagged OVERLAP row:
    PROBE-MERGE plain    appears 1 time(s)
    PROBE-MERGE credit   appears 1 time(s)
    PROBE-MERGE hi       appears 1 time(s)
    PROBE-MERGE flag     appears 0 time(s)
    PROBE-MERGE overlap  appears 1 time(s)
Exactly right: ordinary living rows once, HI rows once, a non-HI flagged row
ZERO times (excluded from the walk), and the HI+flagged overlap row EXACTLY
ONCE — it is dropped from livingOutflows by the HI-first check and picked up
by healthcareOutflows, so it is neither double-counted nor lost.
The same probe quantifies why the merge matters:
    PROBE-MERGE merged signed sum = 1900.00 ; ONE Abs = 1900.00
    PROBE-MERGE two-Abs (attempt-3 shape) would be |living|+|hc| = 3000.00 + 1100.00 = 4100.00
A $2,200 divergence on this fixture — the defect class, measured.
